00:01:28.070 --> 00:01:34.190
important to bear in mind as we finally see
Jimmy rest on one and for once he does the

00:01:34.190 --> 00:01:35.310
right thing.

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:39.960
The colour also gave a clue to what time period
we'd be in and this is actually where we begin

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:40.960
everything.

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:45.920
Now we open with a flashback to Season Bagman
and catch Mike and Jimmy in the desert.

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:50.240
This was one of the standout episodes in the
series and this entry very much feels like

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:56.470
a time machine trip with us getting several
cameos such as Mike, Marie, Walt, Bill Oakley,

00:01:56.470 --> 00:01:59.070
Chuck and a couple of big characters.

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:02.710
Time machines are a major talking point of
the entry and we see Jimmy talking about them

00:02:02.710 --> 00:02:06.710
with Mike and Walt before he picks up the
book by HG Wells when he's with Chuck.

00:02:06.710 --> 00:02:11.350
This opening is a great way to start us off
and after finding a water tank both Jimmy

00:02:11.350 --> 00:02:15.990
and Mike discuss regrets, the future and it's
basically just an introspective look at the

00:02:15.990 --> 00:02:16.990
characters.

00:02:16.990 --> 00:02:21.080
I love how Jimmys water has a suspiciously
yellow liquid in it and it shows how desperate

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:25.220
that he is.

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:28.920
These time machine conversations that appear
throughout all take place at pivotal points

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:33.780
in his life and they're very much intersections
in which we see how his regrets change.

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:39.990
Jimmy says that they could take the money
and he would use six million of it to build

00:02:39.990 --> 00:02:43.900
a time machine.

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:51.360
Mike says that he'd go back to March 17th
1984 aka the day he took his first bribe.

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:55.500
This had a massive impact on the character
as not only did it lead him down the road

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:59.239
to becoming a criminal but it also led to
the death of his son Matt.

00:02:59.239 --> 00:03:03.860
He says he'd also go forward to see how people
were doing, clearly referring to his family

00:03:03.860 --> 00:03:05.670
and those he cares about.

00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:10.080
Jimmy on the other hand is all about the dough
and he'd rather go back in time and invest

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:13.400
the other million into Warren Buffetts Berkshire
Hathaway.

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:18.050
It's very much just the case of using a time
machine to go back and win the lottery because

00:03:18.050 --> 00:03:20.810
all that Jimmy cares about is money.

00:03:20.810 --> 00:03:26.800
Clearly he'd have a lot of things he'd change
but at this point he's so materialistic that

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:29.330
he believes wealth is the key to happiness.

00:03:29.330 --> 00:03:30.810
From here we cut to the titles.

00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:31.810
-

00:03:31.810 --> 00:03:40.989
In this we can catch the isotopes air freshner
from Jeffs cab and this was also something

00:03:40.989 --> 00:03:42.730
that very much led to Genes downfall.

00:03:42.730 --> 00:03:47.280
Had he not got in that cab he probably would've
been stuck as the manager of Cinnabon but

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:50.140
it gave him an avenue where he went back to
old habits.

00:03:50.140 --> 00:03:54.160
I also view this as being the moment that
Jimmy was caught as it set up the dominoes

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:56.019
that led to everything falling apart.

00:03:56.019 --> 00:04:00.380
We then jump immediately to the other side
of last week with us even hearing the door

00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:03.170
close behind him as he bolts out of Marions.&nbsp;

00:04:03.170 --> 00:04:06.280
She describes his car and also the direction
hes going in.

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:11.130
He grabs everything he can from his home and
I love how he has a stack of flip phones in

00:04:11.130 --> 00:04:12.130
his cupboard.

00:04:12.130 --> 00:04:17.849
Not only is this a cool nod to him selling
phones but it also shows how prepared that

00:04:17.849 --> 00:04:20.349
he is incase it all goes t*ts up.

00:04:20.349 --> 00:04:24.279
Jimmy ends up trying to call out to my man
the hoover guy but unfortunately he messes

00:04:24.279 --> 00:04:25.889
up and is caught.

00:04:25.889 --> 00:04:30.270
Now often when you hear of criminal masterminds
being caught you'll hear of how they're found

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:33.409
in some of the worst circumstances trying
to evade the law.

00:04:33.409 --> 00:04:38.039
This is very much the case with Jimmy who
due to his confession will be labelled as

00:04:38.039 --> 00:04:40.839
having one of the biggest drug empires in
America.&nbsp;

00:04:40.839 --> 00:04:44.650
He's found in a dumpster after failing to
escape the law and him stoopings to these

00:04:44.650 --> 00:04:48.180
depths shows how far he's fallen from his
mansions and sexy suits.

00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:52.349
Now in some of the best foreshadowing I've
ever seen, episode 1 in this season actually

00:04:52.349 --> 00:04:55.759
started off with a shot of his cardboard cut
out being put into a dumpster.

00:04:55.759 --> 00:05:00.779
It really shows the attention to detail that
the series carries and how it sets up things

00:05:00.779 --> 00:05:01.889
far in advance.

00:05:01.889 --> 00:05:06.289
I love this moment and it gives me chills
going back to watch this to see how it hinted

00:05:06.289 --> 00:05:11.840
at whats to come.

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:12.840
-

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:19.909
This is echoed in the commercial that we see
the police watching.

00:05:19.909 --> 00:05:25.009
We can hear his over the top bravado narration
about how he can get anyone out of jail whilst

00:05:25.009 --> 00:05:28.289
we see him looking at what would normally
be a one way ticket.

00:05:28.289 --> 00:05:32.809
He gets his one phone call and he uses it
to contact Cinnabon to tell them they'll need

00:05:32.809 --> 00:05:34.059
a new manager.

00:05:34.059 --> 00:05:38.119
Got lots of flashes here of Gus calling up
to make sure los pollos hermanos had a manager

00:05:38.119 --> 00:05:42.389
in place after he was shot and it's nice that
they show the similarities in the characters

00:05:42.389 --> 00:05:44.029
when they're at their lowest points.

00:05:44.029 --> 00:05:48.180
Now Jimmy demands another phonecall after
seeing a carving about a lawyer doing...doing

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:51.310
what and he reaches out to Bill Oakley.

00:05:51.310 --> 00:05:54.539
Better Call Bill spin off coming soon baby.

00:05:54.539 --> 00:05:58.789
As we saw earlier in the season he's now switched
sides to become a defense attorney.

00:05:58.789 --> 00:06:06.629
He ends up taking Jimmys case and I love how
Saul sells him on how big it'll be for him.

00:06:06.629 --> 00:06:07.629
-

00:06:07.629 --> 00:06:14.580
Now at this point we get a cameo by Marie
and the authorities basically want to throw

00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:16.089
the book at him.

00:06:16.089 --> 00:06:20.840
Initially he's facing life plus 190 years
but Saul comes out on top.

00:06:20.840 --> 00:06:24.969
He's initially offered a deal of 30 years
but he asks for Marie to come in after referring

00:06:24.969 --> 00:06:26.880
to her as Schraeders Widow.

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:30.819
Really tragic way to see her labelled like
this and watching her talk about Hank and

00:06:30.819 --> 00:06:34.039
Steve Gomez is really heartbreaking to watch.

00:06:34.039 --> 00:06:37.690
Reminds you of how much they really were the
good guys and how Hank would always come in

00:06:37.690 --> 00:06:40.810
with a smile and a joke even in the darkest
times.

00:06:40.810 --> 00:06:43.470
Miss you man...

00:06:43.470 --> 00:06:44.470
-

00:06:44.470 --> 00:06:49.419
She says that he did it all for money, further
linking back to the first time machine conversation.

00:06:49.419 --> 00:06:54.740
It's so tragic but Saul twists things so that
he's seen as a victim just like her.

00:06:54.740 --> 00:06:58.879
It's incredible watching him go to work and
he mentions the name Mayhew which was the

00:06:58.879 --> 00:07:01.619
surname of Badger from Breaking Bad.

00:07:01.619 --> 00:07:05.469
Saul talks about being taken into the desert
and he basically spins it to say that he feared

00:07:05.469 --> 00:07:06.749
for his life the entire time.

00:07:06.749 --> 00:07:07.749
Walt of course led to the deaths of several
people which Saul has knowledge of.

00:07:07.749 --> 00:07:11.490
He touches upon big moments from Breaking
Bad such as the ten men being murdered in

00:07:11.490 --> 00:07:15.499
prison at the same time and he also brings
up Dan from the series.

00:07:15.499 --> 00:07:20.909
The prosecutor asks if he thinks the Jury
are gonna buy it but he smiles and says.

00:07:20.909 --> 00:07:21.909
-

00:07:21.909 --> 00:07:30.430
Now it turns out that the prosecutor has never
lost a case before and this has given him

00:07:30.430 --> 00:07:35.309
not only an ego but also the fear that the
first L is right around the corner.

00:07:35.309 --> 00:07:39.159
He's well aware that there's potential that
Jimmy can drop this sob story in the court

00:07:39.159 --> 00:07:41.090
and win one of them round.

00:07:41.090 --> 00:07:45.319
Though Marie doesnt want them to negotiate
he ends up working with Jimmy and they cut

00:07:45.319 --> 00:07:47.830
his sentence down to seven years.

00:07:47.830 --> 00:07:52.439
Jimmy is so in control of the entire thing
he even picks not only his prison but also

00:07:52.439 --> 00:07:53.949
the wing that he'll be on.

00:07:53.949 --> 00:07:59.180
He chooses the only federal institution with
a golf programme and brings up Bernie Madoff.

00:07:59.180 --> 00:08:04.789
Incase you don't know, Bernie was the ex-chairman
of the Nasdaq stock exchange and he also ran

00:08:04.789 --> 00:08:13.719
the biggest ponzi scheme in history, totalling
in at a whopping 64 billion.

00:08:13.719 --> 00:08:17.289
Jimmy will do anything to avoid Gen pop and
this is important to bear in mind to show

00:08:17.289 --> 00:08:19.240
what hes sacrificing in the end.

00:08:19.240 --> 00:08:23.889
He gets too arrogant though and in order for
more stuff like ice cream he says he'll offer

00:08:23.889 --> 00:08:25.719
up info on Howard's death.

00:08:25.719 --> 00:08:30.830
However they reveal that Kim already gave
everything up which sweeps Jimmy off his feet.

00:08:30.830 --> 00:08:34.640
Cut to the Walt and Jimmy in the moment before
they were both shipped off to their new lives.

00:08:34.640 --> 00:08:39.039
In the case of both they ended up coming back
and we also get a time machine conversation

00:08:39.039 --> 00:08:40.039
here.

00:08:40.039 --> 00:08:43.640
Walt at one point looks at his watch which
was gifted to him as a present by Jesse in

00:08:43.640 --> 00:08:44.910
Breaking Bad.

00:08:44.910 --> 00:08:49.850
This is a TAG Heuer Monaco that was given
to the Monaco Grand Prix winner in 1969.

00:08:49.850 --> 00:08:53.740
It was also worn by Steve McQueens character
in the 1971 film Le Mans.

00:08:53.740 --> 00:09:02.350
The one Walt has is a 2004 reissue and he
showed it to Skylar saying that a man who

00:09:02.350 --> 00:09:06.200
tried to kill him once ended up giving him
it as a present, this showed that she would

00:09:06.200 --> 00:09:07.290
forgive him one day too.

00:09:07.290 --> 00:09:11.351
Here its used in a number of ways to show
how long theyve been waiting, to tie in with

00:09:11.351 --> 00:09:14.970
the time machine and its got some really nice
symbolism to it.

00:09:14.970 --> 00:09:18.880
Jimmy wants to run the thought experiment
again but Walt wants to cut the BS and he

00:09:18.880 --> 00:09:22.010
disregards it as being scientifically impossible.

00:09:22.010 --> 00:09:27.620
He realises that Saul just wants to talk about
regrets and he brings up Gray Matter.

00:09:27.620 --> 00:09:31.520
Walt blames his old pals for kicking him out
the company because of how rich he'd end up

00:09:31.520 --> 00:09:32.520
becoming.

00:09:32.520 --> 00:09:36.250
This mindstate very much explains why he ends
up tormenting them in the finale of Breaking

00:09:36.250 --> 00:09:39.500
Bad as he sees them as artfully removing him.

00:09:39.500 --> 00:09:46.670
Saul leaps to his feet but hes quickly shut
down.

00:09:46.670 --> 00:09:52.250
Jimmy brings up how he slipped once and hurt
himself and you could say he was slipping

00:09:52.250 --> 00:09:53.250
jimmy.

00:09:53.250 --> 00:09:54.250
-

00:09:54.250 --> 00:10:01.840
Come on that was good, anyway this leads Walt
to say.

00:10:01.840 --> 00:10:02.840
-

00:10:02.840 --> 00:10:06.260
Cut to Jimmy on the plane still using his
mouth to get what he wants and he finds out

00:10:06.260 --> 00:10:07.930
about what Kim gave up.

00:10:07.930 --> 00:10:12.560
He also discovers that Cheryl got the info
too so she can now open up a civil suit.

00:10:12.560 --> 00:10:17.200
This gets Jimmys brain spinning and he says
that he's got more to trade on Kim.

00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:22.110
Cut to her back at the lunch table with her
coworkers and it's clear that she's just not

00:10:22.110 --> 00:10:23.110
happy.

00:10:23.110 --> 00:10:27.450
She ends up walking out of her job and we
follow her as she volunteers at pro-bono law

00:10:27.450 --> 00:10:28.450
firm.

00:10:28.450 --> 00:10:31.780
Though its not much it shows that shes slowly
back on the road to becoming her old self

00:10:31.780 --> 00:10:37.190
again but this is crushed when Suzanne calls
her to tell she may be implicated in the new

00:10:37.190 --> 00:10:38.190
proceedings.

00:10:38.190 --> 00:10:42.280
Really devastating watching her learn and
I was kinda worried that Kims life could end

00:10:42.280 --> 00:10:43.720
up being destroyed too.

00:10:43.720 --> 00:10:46.770
She very much had her redemption arc last
week and handed over everything on Howard

00:10:46.770 --> 00:10:50.710
so seeing things could get worse is realy
devastating.

00:10:50.710 --> 00:10:55.190
We see Saul rock up in the shiniest suit he
could find looking like his old sleazeball

00:10:55.190 --> 00:10:56.190
self.

00:10:56.190 --> 00:10:59.830
However the guilt starts to wash over him
as he sees Kim and Marie and this very much

00:10:59.830 --> 00:11:01.650
plays into his next move.

00:11:01.650 --> 00:11:05.970
He goes under the name Saul Goodman and we
watch as he goes off into his sob story after

00:11:05.970 --> 00:11:08.500
the judge asks why he's getting off so lightly.

00:11:08.500 --> 00:11:13.950
This is verbatim to how he said it in the
prosecution office and it's clearly all an

00:11:13.950 --> 00:11:14.950
act.

00:11:14.950 --> 00:11:18.940
However, he quickly shifts gears and he starts
lying to the court in order to take the fall.

00:11:18.940 --> 00:11:22.060
He says he was the one who helped build up
Walt's drug empire.

00:11:22.060 --> 00:11:26.200
He says he lied about Kims involvement because
he wanted her to come to the court and this

00:11:26.200 --> 00:11:27.200
is very much an apology to her.

00:11:27.200 --> 00:11:30.640
We saw how he tried to save her with Lalo
and this is very much the same thing here

00:11:30.640 --> 00:11:33.170
with him saving the woman he loved.

00:11:33.170 --> 00:11:36.730
Trying not to cry watching my dude doing the
right thing and he does it all to make sure

00:11:36.730 --> 00:11:37.730
that Kim is ok.

00:11:37.730 --> 00:11:38.730
He says that without him that Walt wouldve
been dead or in prison without him and he

00:11:38.730 --> 00:11:39.730
also takes the fall for Hank and Gomez in
some way.

00:11:39.730 --> 00:11:42.820
Next he brings up Howard and his voice cracks
as he credits Kim for starting over again

00:11:42.820 --> 00:11:45.660
and actually doing the right thing in handing
herself in.

00:11:45.660 --> 00:11:50.460
We get some great iconography here with a
focus on the exit sign overhead and he goes

00:11:50.460 --> 00:11:53.000
off to talk about Chuck and how he hurt him.

00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:57.900
He destroyed the law for Chuck which led to
him ending his own life and he finally takes

00:11:57.900 --> 00:11:59.310
responsibility for it.

00:11:59.310 --> 00:12:03.580
The scene then gives us a Chuck flashback
to Jimmy helping him out whilst he still had

00:12:03.580 --> 00:12:05.240
his electricity phobia.

00:12:05.240 --> 00:12:08.460
Chuck tells him that if he doesnt like where
he's heading that there's no shame in going

00:12:08.460 --> 00:12:12.960
back and changing things.

00:12:12.960 --> 00:12:16.620
Chuck picks up his copy of the time machine
and he then goes to bed.

00:12:16.620 --> 00:12:20.840
Now this book also popped up in Episode 1
of this season and we could see it as the

00:12:20.840 --> 00:12:22.170
mansion got raided.

00:12:22.170 --> 00:12:26.390
It's such another bit of amazing foreshadowing
to whats to come and Jimmy very much used

00:12:26.390 --> 00:12:29.410
his confession as a way to go back and change
things.

00:12:29.410 --> 00:12:34.280
Though not literally a time machine it corrected
some of his regrets and we watch as hes taken

00:12:34.280 --> 00:12:35.970
to a terrible prison.

00:12:35.970 --> 00:12:39.720
One of the other prisoners recognises that
he's Saul Goodman and though he corrects this

00:12:39.720 --> 00:12:41.570
to Jimmy, the legend lives on.

00:12:41.570 --> 00:12:46.020
The entire bus starts a chant that brings
a sad smile to Jimmys face and inside the

00:12:46.020 --> 00:12:48.140
prison we cut to him flipping dough.

00:12:48.140 --> 00:12:52.180
The scene here very much resembles the way
that the stuff in Cinnabon was shot showing

00:12:52.180 --> 00:12:56.430
how nomatter what path he took he still would've
ended up doing this.

00:12:56.430 --> 00:12:59.560
Really poetic and we watch as he's called
out to meet Kim.

00:12:59.560 --> 00:13:03.290
This scene mirrors how they'd meet in the
carpark and share cigarettes and we even get

00:13:03.290 --> 00:13:05.340
some similar iconography to that.

00:13:05.340 --> 00:13:09.580
This scene genuinely gave me goosebumps throughout
with it's long pauses and you can feel how

00:13:09.580 --> 00:13:13.810
much they really care about each other but
how they know they'll never get their happy

00:13:13.810 --> 00:13:14.810
ending.

00:13:14.810 --> 00:13:19.620
The flame lights up in another burst of colour
showing how much this old flame brings the

00:13:19.620 --> 00:13:21.170
colour into his life.

00:13:21.170 --> 00:13:30.550
We learn Jimmy is now doing 86 years but with
good behaviour who knows.

00:13:30.550 --> 00:13:36.300
It's bitter sweet as he's still gonna spend
the rest of his life in jail and he knows

00:13:36.300 --> 00:13:37.300
it.

00:13:37.300 --> 00:13:41.910
However he sees her on the outside going off
to now live her life free of the torment she

00:13:41.910 --> 00:13:42.910
couldve faced.

00:13:42.910 --> 00:13:50.310
Jimmy shoots his gun fingers at her as she
departs and I'm not crying...you're crying.

00:13:50.310 --> 00:13:54.180
Anyone whos had a breakup where you just want
the other person to be happy can relate a

00:13:54.180 --> 00:13:58.820
lot to the sorrow displayed here and that
closes out Better Call Saul in what was a

00:13:58.820 --> 00:14:02.000
fitting but also devastating end to the show.

00:14:02.000 --> 00:14:06.140
I think we all knew that Jimmy would get his
comeuppance but we can take some solace in

00:14:06.140 --> 00:14:08.650
knowing that at least Kim will be ok.

00:14:08.650 --> 00:14:12.560
Now as for my thoughts on the series I think
that Better Call Saul always had a difficult

00:14:12.560 --> 00:14:18.340
job because it was obviously a prequel/sequel
to one of the best TV shows of all time.

00:14:18.340 --> 00:14:23.611
However, they managed to do what so many others
fail to and at points I actually think this

00:14:23.611 --> 00:14:24.960
was better than Breaking Bad.

00:14:24.960 --> 00:14:28.650
The early seasons of that are good but I think
it takes a couple of them before we really

00:14:28.650 --> 00:14:33.050
get into what made the show must watch whereas
here it hit the ground running for me early

00:14:33.050 --> 00:14:35.440
on and maintained a consistent level throughout.

00:14:35.440 --> 00:14:39.850
The final season has been no different and
it's arguably had the most impactful moments

00:14:39.850 --> 00:14:43.160
from the entire season playing out almost
back to back at points.

00:14:43.160 --> 00:14:48.490
There was of course Nachos death, Howards,
Lalos, the break up of Kim and Jimmy, Gene

00:14:48.490 --> 00:14:52.900
going back to his old ways and several big
moments that made this at an almost masterpiece

00:14:52.900 --> 00:14:54.440
in storytelling at points.

00:14:54.440 --> 00:14:58.770
I've often said that prequels are difficult
to do because we already know where the story

00:14:58.770 --> 00:15:00.050
eventually ends up.

00:15:00.050 --> 00:15:04.990
However whereas most fail to utilise this,
Better Call Saul does because we worry that

00:15:04.990 --> 00:15:09.300
our favourite characters might die and that
will explain why they're not in Breaking Bad.

00:15:09.300 --> 00:15:13.100
On top of this there's also so many great
performances and memorable characters.

00:15:13.100 --> 00:15:16.810
You can pretty much pick out any member of
the cast and chances are they're gonna have

00:15:16.810 --> 00:15:18.590
hit a home run.

00:15:18.590 --> 00:15:23.210
The actors behind this show really sold everything
and there were so many moments where so little

00:15:23.210 --> 00:15:27.460
was spoken but it spoke so much to what the
characters were going through.

00:15:27.460 --> 00:15:31.100
That's why I really loved it and why I think
the series has been one i'll probably go back

00:15:31.100 --> 00:15:33.700
and rewatch because of the quality it delivered.

